# This directory contains the tools and files to convert NCEI ERSSTv6 monthly netCDF files to fortran binaries and then convert to SBBX format.

Tools:
(A) original GISTEMP fortran code to produce the SBBX (called by coordinator programs)
(B) nc_to_fortran_binary_parallel.py - a standalone tool that converts the NCEI netCDF monthly files to fortran binaries (code is  repeated in the coordinator so it is not a dependency)
(C) ERSSTv6_to_SBBX.py - coordinator program that converts the netCDF monthly files into fortran files and then runs the three fortran programs with the correct arguments (alternatively, run (B) and then run updateERSST.sh)


Use instructions:
1) Must modify the paths in toolconfig.py
2) Compile the fortran programs by running `make` in the current folder (requires gfortran). Note: I've tweaked the compile flags a bit.
    Any warnings (like compile warnings that are "deleted feature" seemingly can be ignored)
3) Make sure the monthly ersstv6 files are already downloaded and complete (use tool/download_sst.py for this)
4) run python3 ERSSTv6_to_SBBX.py

After this you should have SBBX.ERSSTv6 in this folder

Notes:
There are nominal discrepancies with the GISTEMP version that would show up in a text conversion tool (the differences are actually on order of 1e-5, but show up in a few places in the text file since solely due to rounding to 0.01).
This was verified by using ../tool/compare_sbbx.py with a ERSSTv5 version of this tool (not provided) and other manual examinations and comparisons of the last official ERSSTv5 SBBX provided (valid month of June 2026).
This seems unavoidable due to a combination of the use of a modern fortran compiler and a different environment (processor) in comparison with how the official SBBX is generated.
The same effect is seen with the pure python version in ../ersstv6py, which produces an output that is identical to this program.

For reference, the current output looks like this:

